A||||9||lennyb4||Switching over gages?||||5||
Z||000000||lennyb4||06-21-2000||01:05 PM||||Ive read the post about changing the crystal to up the speedo. I know the differances between the stock and the SE & GT clusters. Now my question is this: I have a stock 87 Coupe (85 mph speedo) can i put a gage cluster off of a SE or GT and if i can will all the gages read correctly?<BR> TIA||||reg||5
Z||000001||terryk||06-21-2000||01:57 PM||||If you are talking about just plugging in a 120 MPH speedo in place of an 85, just bolt it in. A new crystal (or crystal eliminator) is needed only when you change the face to some strange scale (like 200 MPH, lord only knows why).<P>The tach on the other hand needs to be for a V6 or L4. Otherwise, the crystal needs to be changed to make them read correctly.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||lennyb4||06-21-2000||02:49 PM||||Yea. Theres a bone yard by me that has a couple of SE's. I want to replace my complete cluster with one. I like the higher speedo and the different tach. As for the oil gage (i know the SE & GT didnt have them below the tach) i have a set of rally gages so i dont have to worry about that. BUT what crystal are you refering to? Where is it located? And could i use one from one of the other cars in the bone yard? TIA guys....||||reg||1
Z||000003||dman||06-21-2000||02:51 PM||||Hi lennyb4 and terryk,<P>I hope this doesn't sound like I'm nitpicking, but isn't the crystal for the speedometer, while the tach uses an resistor/capacitor network?<P>So changing the face on a speedo requires a crystal change, and changing your 4cylinder to a 6 or 8 cylinder requires adding (in parallel to increase...or perhaps replacing to descrease) a capacitor.<P>So lennyb4, if the cluster you have is from a 6 (and given that it is a 120mph speedo I'm gonna guess that it is), and your recipient car is also a 6, then everything is cool. Plug and play. However if your recipient car is a 4 or 8 cylinder then you will probably need to solder on a new cap (quite easy actually).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||lennyb4||06-21-2000||03:31 PM||||My car has the iron duck (4 cyl.) and the gage cluster is out of a six cyl. I was following you pretty good until you got to the soldering a cap part? Trust me when i say im real good at working on cars (mechaniclly and some electrical) but dumb as a rock when it comes to stuff like resistors, capacitors, diodes, etc..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||06-21-2000||04:50 PM||||lenny,<P>once you get the cluster out you'll see it's not a big deal to move one gauge. the whole thing is just plug in "boxes" held by clips and screws. goes together like an erector set.<P>the Odo trip and speedo are all seperate parts. as is the tach. keep the old tach and odometer. use the rest in the new pod if you want.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||06-21-2000||05:47 PM||||Lenny,<BR>Ive got an 88 coupe, with the duck, that I've done this to. <BR>The easiest way I found to get the tach to read correctly was to remove the circuit board from my original tach and put it on the replacement. It only involves removing a handful of stamped metal nuts. You won't have an oil gauge on your new tach, so you won't need several of the nuts. Although the tach faces are different, the actual meter movement is the same.<BR>I tried installing the "correct" value capacitor on the SE circuit board, but when I started it up, the tach read TWICE what it should have. The only reason I can think of for this is the DIS ignition. It has to fire each coil pack twice per revolution. <BR>This should only apply to 87 and 88 Ducks, with the DIS.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||terryk||06-21-2000||10:52 PM||||Dman, you're right. On the tach, I was thinking about a Grand Am tach I mod'd for a guy. Different beast.<P>The Fiero tach uses the National meter driver (GM XXXX4339. I don't remember the first numbers). If you cut the traces to pins 5 and 8, you can install a pot and adjust full scale. You can do that on many of the speedo's, but the odo and trip will be wrong. That's why I did the VCO circuit to replace the crystal.<P>Terry||||reg||1
Z||000008||lennyb4||06-22-2000||09:01 AM||||Raydar..So from what you have said i could just switch over the printed circuit board from the original to the up-graded six tach? If thats the case then thats what i'll do. It sounds simple enough and i really do like the style of the six cyl. tach. I'll probally do this early next month and i'll let you guys know how it all works out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>                   Thanks guys,<BR>                        lenny||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||06-22-2000||11:10 AM||||Terryk, <BR>By installing the pot, can you then forget about replacing the cap? <BR>Is it touchy enough to require a multi-turn pot? <BR>What value did you use?<P>Lenny, that's all there is to it. Good luck.<P>BTW... I noticed that the ceramic pieces (resistors?) that are bridged across the back of the gauges will sometimes get a bad connection where they contact the pins.  If you have any gauges that go full scale, and you know the reading is wrong, this would be a good thing to check. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 06-22-2000).]||||reg||1
